(arrangement, flank) pairs        : 4200
  cycles with NO clear opening    : 231  (5.5%)
  cycles with at least one        : 3969

density of CLEAR openings on the cycle, where non-empty:
  min 0.0294   10th 0.1154   median 0.1887   90th 0.3000   max 1.0000

the SAME data read as an arc from k = 0, which is how this session reported it:
  first clear opening: min 0  median 3  90th 11  max 35

Both describe the same cycles. The density is a property OF the cycle; the first hit
is a property of the cycle AND of where you decided to start counting.

========================================================================
WHAT THE REFRAMING CHANGES

  Read as an arc: 'the first clear opening is at median 3, and the required k is
  UNBOUNDED -- a hill-climb reached 369'. That unboundedness is across arrangements
  with different periods. WITHIN an arrangement k < P always, because the verdict
  repeats. So 'k is unbounded' was measuring the origin, not the object.

  Read as a cycle: 5.5% of (arrangement, flank) cycles have NO clear opening -- the
  flank is shadowed. Where a flank is free at all, roughly a FIFTH of its cycle is
  clear (median 18.9%, 10th percentile 11.5%). The clear set is not a needle.

  These are the same 4,200 cycles. The density is a property OF the cycle; the first
  hit is a property of the cycle AND of where counting began.

  The 5.5% are the same phenomenon as the d_min flank saturating below 100% measured
  earlier (89.0% integer, 75.8% small-denominator): a shadowed flank is an EMPTY
  cycle, not a distant first hit. No amount of searching further reaches it, which is
  why widening the search never helped and choosing another flank always did.

  So the statement to aim at is not 'the first clear opening is near'. It is that the
  clear sets of a runner's flanks are not ALL empty -- which is section 4.6's own open
  item, 'not every flank of a runner can be shadowed at once', in cycle language.
